# 5. GOLD AND THE ECONOMY

Gold should be one of the most important resources in the entire game.

The player should have to explore, collect treasure, defeat pirates and trade goods to earn enough gold to build a large and powerful ship.

### Gold system

Create a fully functional gold currency system.

Display the player's current gold balance in the HUD and inventory.

Gold can be obtained through:
- Searching shipwrecks.
- Discovering treasure chests.
- Defeating pirate ships.
- Looting captured enemy vessels.
- Finding abandoned treasure.
- Trading valuable cargo with merchant boats.
- Completing special encounters or discoveries.

Gold must have actual value and uses.

The player should spend gold to:
- Purchase wood, iron, rope and cloth.
- Purchase food and fresh water.
- Buy cannons and weapons.
- Buy cannonballs and gunpowder.
- Purchase better sails.
- Buy ship upgrades.
- Expand the hull and decks.
- Purchase navigation equipment.
- Hire additional crew members when available.
- Purchase rare construction materials.
- Repair their ship through merchants.

The best ship upgrades should require a combination of gold and physical resources. Gold alone should not magically construct the ship.

### Different treasure values

The amount of gold found should vary significantly depending on what is discovered.

A small wreck might contain only a handful of coins, whereas a large merchant ship might contain several valuable chests.

Pirate vessels should also carry different amounts of gold depending on their size, crew and type.

Make treasure discoveries feel rewarding without making gold so common that progression becomes meaningless.

The player should have to make decisions about what to spend their money on.

